#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:1
msgid "Lifetime Safety Analysis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:7
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr "Introduction"

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:9
msgid ""
"Clang Lifetime Safety Analysis is a C++ language extension which warns about "
"potential dangling pointer defects in code. The analysis aims to detect when "
"a pointer, reference or view type (such as `std::string_view`) refers to an "
"object that is no longer alive, a condition that leads to use-after-free "
"bugs and security vulnerabilities. Common examples include pointers to stack "
"variables that have gone out of scope, pointers to heap objects that have "
"been freed, fields holding views to stack-allocated objects (dangling-"
"field), returning pointers/references to stack variables (return stack "
"address) or iterators into container elements invalidated by container "
"operations (e.g., `std::vector::push_back`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:20
msgid ""
"The analysis design is inspired by [Polonius, the Rust borrow checker]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/polonius), but adapted to C++ idioms and "
"constraints, such as the lack of exclusivity enforcement (alias-xor-"
"mutability). Further details on the analysis method can be found in the [RFC "
"on Discourse](https://discourse.llvm.org/t/rfc-intra-procedural-lifetime-"
"analysis-in-clang/86291/)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:24
msgid ""
"This is compile-time analysis; there is no run-time overhead. It tracks "
"pointer validity through intra-procedural data-flow analysis. While it does "
"not require lifetime annotations to get started, in their absence, the "
"analysis treats function calls optimistically, assuming no lifetime effects, "
"thereby potentially missing dangling pointer issues. As more functions are "
"annotated with attributes like [clang::lifetimebound](https://clang.llvm.org/"
"docs/AttributeReference.html#lifetimebound), [gsl::Owner](https://clang.llvm."
"org/docs/AttributeReference.html#gsl-owner), and [gsl::Pointer](https://"
"clang.llvm.org/docs/AttributeReference.html#gsl-pointer), the analysis can "
"see through these lifetime contracts and enforce lifetime safety at call "
"sites with higher accuracy. This approach supports gradual adoption in "
"existing codebases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:34
msgid ""
"This analysis is designed for bug finding, not verification. It may miss "
"some lifetime issues and can produce false positives. It does not guarantee "
"the absence of all lifetime bugs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:39
msgid "Getting Started"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:55
msgid ""
"This example demonstrates a basic use-after-scope bug. The `std::"
"string_view` object `v` holds a reference to `s`, a `std::string`. The "
"lifetime of `s` ends at the end of the inner block, causing `v` to become a "
"dangling reference. The analysis flags the assignment `v = s` as defective "
"because `s` is destroyed while `v` is still alive and points to `s`, and "
"adds a note to where `v` is used after `s` has been destroyed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:63
msgid "Running The Analysis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:65
msgid ""
"To run the analysis, compile with the `-Wlifetime-safety-permissive` flag, e."
"g."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:71
msgid ""
"This flag enables a core set of lifetime safety checks. For more fine-"
"grained control over warnings, see {ref}`warning_flags`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:74
msgid "Lifetime Annotations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:76
msgid ""
"While lifetime analysis can detect many issues without annotations, its "
"precision increases significantly when types and functions are annotated "
"with lifetime contracts. These annotations clarify ownership semantics and "
"lifetime dependencies, enabling the analysis to reason more accurately about "
"pointer validity across function calls."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:82
msgid "Owner and Pointer Types"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:84
msgid ""
"Lifetime analysis distinguishes between types that own the data they point "
"to (Owners) and types that are non-owning views or references to data owned "
"by others (Pointers). This distinction is made using GSL-style attributes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:88
msgid ""
"`[[gsl::Owner]]`: For types that manage the lifetime of a resource, like "
"`std::string`, `std::vector`, `std::unique_ptr`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:90
msgid ""
"`[[gsl::Pointer]]`: For non-owning types that borrow resources, like `std::"
"string_view`, or raw pointers (which are implicitly treated as pointers)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:94
msgid ""
"Many common STL types, such as `std::string_view` and container iterators, "
"are automatically recognized as Pointers or Owners. You can annotate your "
"own types using these attributes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:125
msgid ""
"Without these annotations, the analysis may not be able to determine whether "
"a type is owning or borrowing, which can affect analysis precision. For more "
"details on these attributes, see the Clang attribute reference for [gsl::"
"Owner](https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AttributeReference.html#gsl-owner) and "
"[gsl::Pointer](https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AttributeReference.html#gsl-"
"pointer)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:132
msgid ""
"Types with mixed ownership semantics (owning some data while holding views "
"to other data) or types with multiple view fields with different lifetimes "
"should not be annotated. The analysis does not yet support expressing such "
"nuanced lifetime relationships. Future enhancements, such as named "
"lifetimes, may provide better support for these patterns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:140
msgid "LifetimeBound"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:142
msgid ""
"The `[[clang::lifetimebound]]` attribute can be applied to function "
"parameters or to the implicit `this` parameter of a method (by placing it "
"after the method declarator). It indicates that the returned value becomes "
"invalid when the attributed parameter or `this` object is destroyed. This is "
"crucial for functions that return views or references to their arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:167
msgid ""
"Without `[[clang::lifetimebound]]` on `getView()`, the analysis would not "
"know that the value returned by `getView()` depends on the temporary "
"`MyOwner` object, and it would not be able to diagnose the dangling `sv`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:171
msgid ""
"The analysis also tracks record types returned from functions and "
"constructors with `[[clang::lifetimebound]]` annotated parameters:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:197
msgid ""
"For more details, see [lifetimebound](https://clang.llvm.org/docs/"
"AttributeReference.html#lifetimebound)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:199
msgid "NoEscape"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:203
msgid ""
"The `[[clang::noescape]]` attribute can be applied to function parameters of "
"pointer or reference type. It indicates that the function will not allow the "
"parameter to escape its scope, for example, by returning it or assigning it "
"to a field or global variable. This is useful for parameters passed to "
"callbacks or visitors that are only used during the call and not stored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:209
msgid ""
"For more details, see [noescape](https://clang.llvm.org/docs/"
"AttributeReference.html#noescape)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LifetimeSafety.md:211
msgid "Checks Performed"
msgstr ""

